     1	// Deferred work: registertimer / removetimer, driven from the OnTick hook.
     2	//
     3	// This is the replacement for DelayCommand, and that is not a convenience --
     4	// routine 7 DelayCommand takes an `action`, which the NWScript compiler emits
     5	// as STORE_STATE bytecode capturing a closure. There is no value a Lua script
     6	// can push that the VM will accept there. DelayCommand, AssignCommand (6) and
     7	// ActionDoCommand (294) are the three K1 routines the bridge cannot marshal,
     8	// and this is what a script uses instead.
     9	//
    10	//   local id = kp.registertimer(1500, "on_bang", { n = 3 })
    11	//   function on_bang(id, count, data)
    12	//       kp.feedback("bang " .. count)
    13	//       return count < data.n            -- true renews, anything else stops
    14	//   end
    15	//   kp.removetimer(id)
    16	//
    17	// The callback is either a STRING or a FUNCTION.
    18	//
    19	//   A name is resolved at fire time against the script's globals, so a typo is
    20	//   invisible until the timer expires, and redefining the global changes what
    21	//   the next firing runs.
    22	//
    23	//   A function is held by a registry reference from registration until the
    24	//   timer ends, so a closure keeps its upvalues across firings:
    25	//
    26	//     local hits = 0
    27	//     kp.registertimer(500, function(id, count) hits = hits + 1; return count < 3 end)
    28	//
    29	//   The reference is released exactly once: on completion (anything but true
    30	//   or 1 returned), on removetimer, on a throw, or with the state when it
    31	//   closes. A function value cannot leak into the next generation of a
    32	//   reloaded script, because timers never cross a state close (below).
    33	//   Errors from a function timer are charged to "function <source>:<line>",
    34	//   the place the function was defined, so every timer made from one closure
    35	//   definition shares one error budget, as every timer naming one global does.
    36	//   A C function (kp.registertimer(ms, error)) has no source line, so it is
    37	//   charged to "function [C] <address>": one budget per C function.
    38	//
    39	//   A name is looked up inside a protected call, so a strict-globals __index
    40	//   on _G that raises is an ordinary timer error: reported, charged to the
    41	//   name, and the timer is cancelled. It is not counted as a firing.
    42	//
    43	// Anything else in the callback slot is an argument error at the call site.
    44	//
    45	// THE DELAY is a finite number of milliseconds from 0 to kMaxDelayMs
    46	// (2^32 - 1, about 49.7 days). NaN, infinity, negatives and anything larger
    47	// are an argument error; they used to be accepted and, for infinity, fire at
    48	// once.
    49	// Both forms share one scheduler, one id space and the same arguments
    50	// (id, count, data).
    51	//
    52	// SCHEDULING. Timers fire from pump(), which the OnTick hook body calls once
    53	// per frame with a millisecond clock. Everything due at that instant is
    54	// collected first and fired afterwards, one at a time, with each timer looked
    55	// up again immediately before it runs -- a callback is allowed to register
    56	// timers, cancel timers, or get its own script quarantined, and none of those
    57	// may invalidate the loop that is walking them. A renewed timer is rescheduled
    58	// from now, not from when it was due, so a frame hitch does not turn into a
    59	// burst of catch-up firings.
    60	//
    61	// OWNERSHIP AND RELOAD. A timer belongs to one lua_State. Ids are unique across
    62	// the whole process, but removetimer only matches ids belonging to the calling
    63	// state, so one script cannot cancel another's timer. Ids live with the
    64	// lua_State that registered them.
    65	//
    66	// A reload drops that script's timers, and the script re-registers whatever it
    67	// wants when its chunk runs again. This is the sensible answer rather than the
    68	// lazy one: a timer holds a Lua registry reference to its userdata and names a
    69	// global function, and both of those live in the lua_State that a reload
    70	// closes. Carrying an id across generations would mean handing a script back an
    71	// id whose callback and userdata are gone, and letting removetimer(id) cancel a
    72	// timer belonging to a different generation of the same file. Re-registration
    73	// at the top of the chunk is a line of Lua; a timer that survives into a state
    74	// where its callback does not exist is a bug that fires later, somewhere else.
    75	//
    76	// Nothing here needs the runtime to remember to clean up. Each state carries a
    77	// finalised sentinel in its own registry, so lua_close -- from unload, reload
    78	// or quarantine -- drops that state's timers on the way out. Once the sentinel
    79	// has been finalised, registertimer on that state raises: a script's own __gc
    80	// can run after the sentinel's during lua_close, and a timer registered there
    81	// would otherwise outlive its state.
    82	//
    83	// TWO DIVERGENCES FROM data/hostapi.toml, which declares this surface as
    84	// kp.timer.* and has no generator yet. That file makes cancelling the running
    85	// timer a no-op; here it raises, because removing a timer from inside its own
    86	// callback is a bug a script should see at the call site. A throwing callback
    87	// is fatal to that timer: the error is charged to the script's budget AND the
    88	// timer is cancelled, so quarantine is reached across several timers rather
    89	// than by one repeater failing over and over. Cancelling is what actually
    90	// stops the forever-traceback; the budget is what stops the script.
    91	//
    92	// The same file corroborates the renewal rule independently: "a slow frame does
    93	// not fire the timer twice to catch up -- it fires once and the next due time
    94	// is measured from now."
    95	#pragma once
    96	
    97	#include <cstddef>
    98	#include <cstdint>
    99	
   100	struct lua_State;
   101	
   102	namespace kp::api {
   103	
   104	// Installs kp.registertimer and kp.removetimer into this state, and the
   105	// sentinel that drops this state's timers when it closes.
   106	void register_timers(lua_State*);
   107	
   108	namespace timers {
   109	
   110	// The largest delay kp.registertimer accepts, in milliseconds.
   111	inline constexpr std::uint64_t kMaxDelayMs = 0xFFFFFFFFull;
   112	
   113	// Fire everything due at `now_ms`. Returns how many callbacks ran.
   114	//
   115	// `game_tick` is stamped onto any error a callback raises, so a script error
   116	// from a timer reads the same as one from an event callback. The OnTick body
   117	// has both numbers to hand, and calls this after it dispatches OnTick:
   118	//
   119	//     h.dispatch(*ev, args);
   120	//     kp::api::timers::pump(kp::api::timers::now_ms(), h.tick());
   121	//     h.advance_tick();
   122	//
   123	// After dispatch rather than before, so a script that registers a timer from
   124	// OnTick does not have that timer fire on the same frame.
   125	unsigned pump(std::uint64_t now_ms, std::uint64_t game_tick);
   126	
   127	// A monotonic millisecond clock, for a caller that has no better one. Monotonic
   128	// on purpose: a wall clock that steps backwards would leave every timer due.
   129	std::uint64_t now_ms();
   130	
   131	// The scheduler's notion of "now", which is the caller's to define.
   132	//
   133	// registertimer schedules at clock() + delay and pump() sets clock() to the
   134	// time it was handed, so the two always agree. That matters more than it looks:
   135	// if registration read a real clock while pump was driven from a synthetic one,
   136	// every timer would be due immediately or never, and the difference would only
   137	// show up outside the game. Until someone sets it, clock() is now_ms().
   138	void          set_clock(std::uint64_t now_ms);
   139	std::uint64_t clock();
   140	
   141	// A callback that throws counts against its script's error budget and is
   142	// quarantined at the same threshold as any event callback. The budget lives in
   143	// HostConfig, which has no accessor, so the runtime passes it here. The default
   144	// matches HostConfig's own.
   145	void set_error_budget(unsigned);
   146	
   147	// Drop every timer belonging to this state. The finaliser calls this, so the
   148	// runtime does not have to; it is exposed for a caller that wants to cancel a
   149	// script's timers without closing it.
   150	//
   151	// Registry references held by those timers (data and function callbacks) are
   152	// not released, because the only caller that matters is a state on its way out
   153	// and releasing them there would touch a half-torn-down state. Calling this on
   154	// a live state leaks those refs until the state closes.
   155	void forget_state(lua_State*);
   156	
   157	// How many timers are scheduled, in total or for one state. Diagnostics.
   158	std::size_t pending();
   159	std::size_t pending(lua_State*);
   160	
   161	}  // namespace timers
   162	}  // namespace kp::api
     1	// kp.vec: a small 3D vector type for Lua scripts.
     2	//
     3	//   local p = kp.vec(nwscript.call("GetPosition", kp.obj.pc():id()))
     4	//   local q = p + kp.vec(0, 0, 2)
     5	//   local d = p:distance(q)                      -- 2
     6	//   nwscript.call("SetFacingPoint", q)          -- still a vector to NWScript
     7	//
     8	// A vec is an ordinary table with numeric fields x, y and z, the same shape the
     9	// routine bridge already reads and returns for an NWScript `vector`. That is
    10	// the whole compatibility story: kp.vec adds a shared metatable and nothing
    11	// the bridge has to know about, and a plain {x=, y=, z=} table still marshals
    12	// exactly as before.
    13	//
    14	// CONSTRUCTION. kp.vec(x, y, z) takes exactly three finite numbers.
    15	// kp.vec(t) copies x, y and z out of any table that has them, which is how a
    16	// routine's plain vector result (or another vec) becomes a vec. Strings are
    17	// not coerced, and NaN or infinity is an error at the call that produced it
    18	// rather than a position that silently never matches anything.
    19	//
    20	// VALUES. Every operation returns a FRESH vec and never modifies its operands.
    21	// The fields stay ordinary and writable (v.z = v.z + 1 is fine); because of
    22	// that, every operation re-checks that x, y and z are finite numbers when it
    23	// reads them. Results are checked too, scalars included: finite inputs can
    24	// still overflow (kp.vec(1e155, 0, 0):length() squares past the largest
    25	// double), and such an operation raises "result is not finite" rather than
    26	// returning inf.
    27	//
    28	// OPERATORS. a + b, a - b, a * n, n * a, a / n, -a, a == b, tostring(a).
    29	// An operand that is not a vec may be any table with finite x, y, z. Scaling
    30	// takes a finite number; vec * vec is an error (use dot or cross), as is
    31	// dividing by zero. Equality compares coordinates exactly, and only between
    32	// two vecs: Lua 5.1 consults __eq only when both sides share it, so a plain
    33	// table is never == a vec.
    34	//
    35	// METHODS. length, lengthsq, normalize, dot, cross, distance, distancesq,
    36	// lerp(b, t), copy, unpack. normalize() of the zero vector returns a fresh
    37	// zero vector rather than NaNs. normalize() of a nonzero vector whose length
    38	// overflows or underflows raises rather than returning zero. lerp does not
    39	// clamp t.
    40	//
    41	// NAMES. None of these, nor `vec`, equals an NWScript routine name, compared
    42	// case-insensitively (kp_vectortest checks this against the compiled
    43	// catalogue). x, y and z match only the parameter names of routine Vector,
    44	// and they are required: they are the field names the bridge marshals.
    45	//
    46	// The metatable is shared by every vec in a state and protected:
    47	// getmetatable(v) returns "kp.vec" and setmetatable(v, ...) raises.
    48	#pragma once
    49	
    50	struct lua_State;
    51	
    52	namespace kp::api {
    53	
    54	// Installs kp.vec into this state.
    55	void register_vector(lua_State*);
    56	
    57	}  // namespace kp::api

